1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:15,300
Hi, I'm [your name]. This is AP Autopilot, my solution to problem statement one: invoice processing, from PDF to decision. Every invoice goes through eight stages, from intake to a final decision.

2
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:25,100
Gemini reads the document; fixed rules decide: approve, review, or reject. I designed four edge cases, and I'll run each one live.

3
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:34,900
Drop in any PDF, digital or scanned, or pick a prepared scenario: two happy paths, four edge cases, six exceptions.

4
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:49,500
Let's start with a clean invoice from Apex Office Supplies. Watch the pipeline on the left: it checks the file, reads the text layer, and extracts every field.

5
00:00:49,700 --> 00:01:04,700
Approved: all twenty-nine checks passed, and the next step is routed to the AP supervisor, with payment scheduled on Net 30. Every field shows a confidence score and the text it came from.

6
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:16,800
In the PO match tab, every invoice line maps to the purchase order. Quantity, price and tax are all within tolerance: a clean two-way match.

7
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:28,800
Second happy path: Northwind bills one PO in three monthly instalments. The PO number is written oddly and the dates are day-first, but it still resolves.

8
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:41,200
It matches against what's left on the PO, not the full value. This is invoice two of three, so it's approved, and the PO is now sixty-seven percent used.

9
00:01:41,400 --> 00:02:04,100
Edge case one: a scanned invoice with no PO number at all. There's no text layer, so it runs OCR, at ninety-three percent confidence, and then it has to work out which purchase order this belongs to. It goes to manual review.

10
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:12,000
Here's the original scan beside the text the system read.

11
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:23,200
It scored Cobalt's open POs: a hundred percent match versus sixteen. Strong, but an inferred PO still needs the buyer's confirmation.

12
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:46,500
Edge case two is about duplicates. Apex re-sent an invoice we already paid, as a payment reminder, with a new date and the number re-formatted with a leading zero. Normalised, it's the same invoice, so it's rejected before it can be paid twice.

13
00:02:46,700 --> 00:03:12,500
But the opposite mistake is just as costly. Summit bills the same four thousand five hundred dollars every month, so a naive check would block it. The system sees a different service period, recognises recurring billing, and approves it.

14
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:45,800
Edge case three: Northwind's final instalment. The agreed rate is sixteen thousand a month, but this invoice says nineteen and a half thousand, with a surcharge bundled in. The bar shows earlier invoices in grey, this one in amber, and the overrun in red. That's a twenty-one point nine percent price variance, so it goes to the buyer, by name, for a PO amendment or a corrected invoice.

15
00:03:46,000 --> 00:04:15,200
Edge case four is the expensive one: vendor impersonation. The PO and the amounts are perfect. But it says Limited instead of Inc, the tax ID digits are swapped, the email domain is a look-alike, and it asks us to pay a new bank account. Two or more identity mismatches mean a fraud hold: don't pay, and call the vendor on the number we already have on file.

16
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:32,800
Back to the scanned invoice in the review queue. The buyer has confirmed the PO, so I add a note and approve. My name, the decision and the reason all go into the audit trail.

17
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:50,600
Finally, the dashboard: every invoice, its decision and why, with the value cleared and the value blocked. Gemini reads, rules decide, and every outcome can be explained. Thanks for watching.
